    "abstract": "Semi-arid Cear\u00e1 (NE Brazil) is highly sensitive to climate variability, yet the non-stationary, multi-scale nature of its hydroclimate hampers traditional analyses and forecasting. To address this complexity, this study aims to characterize the multi-scale variability of rainfall and streamflow and quantify their lagged teleconnections with global climate drivers. Monthly rainfall and streamflow for 1987\u20132022 were decomposed with Complete Ensemble Empirical. Mode Decomposition with Adaptive Noise (CEEMDAN) to isolate intrinsic mode functions (IMFs) and a residual trend. Teleconnections with 16 climate indices were quantified via cross-correlation (lags 0\u201312 months) and bidirectional stepwise regression using the top-correlated candidates per IMF; index-selection frequencies were summarized irrespective of lag. Interannual variability (IMFs 3\u20136) was dominated by ENSO-family indices, especially NINO4/NINO12/ONI and BEST, while low-frequency variability and residual trends were primarily linked to Atlantic modes, with AMO unsmoothed most prominent. Spatially, Pacific influence was stronger at coastal gauges, whereas interior basins were more strongly conditioned by low-frequency Atlantic variability. These scale-aware relationships have operational value: TNA\u2013TSA gradient supports within-season decisions, ENSO indices inform annual planning, and AMO unsmoothed provides multi-year context for carryover storage and drought readiness. The findings provide an organizing principle for forecast-informed water management in Cear\u00e1.",
    "field": "Hydroclimatic teleconnections",
    "problem": "The non-stationary, multi-scale nature of hydroclimate in semi-arid Cear\u00e1 hampers traditional analyses, making it difficult to attribute rainfall and streamflow variability to specific global climate drivers and develop robust, scale-aware forecasts for water management.",
    "method": "This study proposes a method combining CEEMDAN decomposition with cross-correlation and stepwise regression to characterize the multi-scale variability of rainfall and streamflow and quantify their lagged teleconnections with global climate drivers.",
    "task": "The task is to characterize the multi-scale variability of rainfall and streamflow in semi-arid Cear\u00e1, Brazil, and to quantify their lagged teleconnections with global climate drivers using a decomposition-based statistical analysis.",
